# Repo files that aren't site content (2026-08-07). The Netlify publish
# directory is the repo root, so these were being served publicly —
# CONTENT_PLAN.md was live at cancuntogo.com/CONTENT_PLAN.md.
# The trailing ! forces the rule; without it Netlify serves the real file
# and ignores the rule entirely. Shadowing, not deletion — the files stay
# on the CDN, they're just no longer routable.
/CONTENT_PLAN.md                         /404.html                               404!
/README.md                               /404.html                               404!
/CLAUDE.md                               /404.html                               404!
/package.json                            /404.html                               404!
/package-lock.json                       /404.html                               404!
/data/*                                  /404.html                               404!

# Build tooling, not site content (2026-09-02). Same exposure as the block
# above: the publish directory is the repo root, so scripts/url-inspect.mjs
# was live and publishing the GSC account email, the domain-property detail
# and the gcloud scopes it needs. No keys are in the repo — the token comes
# from local ADC at runtime — but none of it belongs on a crawlable URL, and
# nothing on the site links to these files.
/scripts/*                               /404.html                               404!

# Marketing templates and their source aren't site content (2026-09-15). Same
# exposure as the blocks above — the publish directory is the repo root, so
# marketing/email-header.html, the Instagram templates, brand-kit.js, export.mjs
# and the README were all live and crawlable. Nothing on the site links to them.
#
# marks/ is the deliberate exception. README.md documents those transparent
# logo PNGs as hosted assets "for email / Canva / decks", and
# email-header.html points at cancuntogo.com/marketing/marks/*.png by absolute
# URL — so force-404ing them would break the logo in email already sent. The
# 200! keeps them served; it has to come first, because first match wins.
/marketing/marks/*                       /marketing/marks/:splat                 200!
/marketing/*                             /404.html                               404!
